WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.560
Site Director Tilda Moose was staring down
the barrel of the gun that would cause the

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.860
apocalypse, and SCP-6000 was pulling back
the hammer.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:15.259
Typically, someone as important as the Site
Director of Site-19 - the Foundation’s largest

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:20.320
containment site - would never be put on a
mission as risky as accompanying MTF Sigma-3,

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.690
aka The Bibliographers, right into the heart
of an active anomaly.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:29.730
But these were extraordinary circumstances
to say the least.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:35.610
Dr. Moose and the operatives of MTF Sigma-3
Fireteam Chicago, entered Brazilian airspace

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.430
in late December of the year 2030.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.070
Below them, an ocean of trees.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:46.860
The Amazon Rainforest, known to some as the
Lung of the World - and that lung had a cancer

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.760
known as SCP-6000.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.829
Director Moose could see it on the horizon,
beautiful and terrifying: Like a huge, shimmering

00:00:55.829 --> 00:00:58.320
scar on the jungle’s canopy.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.820
It had been identified a few days earlier
via live satellite imaging, opening up like

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.050
a wound in the world’s largest rainforest.

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:12.060
And it was growing, swallowing up and affecting
entire areas of land by the kilometer.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.670
Not wanting to waste anymore time, The Foundation
immediately sent in a recon team to gather

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.400
the first intel on this rapidly developing
anomaly.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.220
Once inside the affected area, known to the
Foundation as “The Exclusion Zone”, they

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:30.240
discovered unprecedented phenomena: The flora
and fauna of the rainforest had been altered

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.850
drastically in the affected areas.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:38.440
Trees appeared to be somehow naturally carved
into living bookshelves, their leaves tattooed

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.800
with words.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.190
Huge alien birds soared above the forest with
their paper-like wings, also covered in text.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:50.550
Spotted jaguars with six or even eight limbs,
like a freakish cross-breeding of animal and

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:55.510
insect, loped through the papery thickets
in hunt of word-covered prey.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:02:00.940
Every living creature in the forest had been
transformed by SCP-6000, and it was about

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:03.270
to get a whole lot worse.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.930
If SCP-6000’s Exclusion Zone continued to
expand, not only would the anomalous effects

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:15.750
prolong, but they’d increase in their intensity,
mutating everything SCP-6000 touched, reaching

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.620
further and further across the landscape.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:23.360
The Foundation needed to gather all the intel
they possibly could on this emerging anomaly

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.750
before it was too late, but time was beginning
to run out.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:33.910
Just as they began to gain traction, the Foundation
suddenly lost contact with the initial reconnaissance

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:34.910
team.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:38.230
They seemed to have fallen off the map, vanished.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:43.451
This triggered the immediate deployment of
subsequent expeditions into the SCP-6000 exclusion

00:02:43.451 --> 00:02:44.451
zone.

00:02:44.451 --> 00:02:48.610
When the Bibliographers’ first strike team
arrived on the scene of the Exclusion Zone’s

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:53.980
bizarre, alien world in search of the missing
reconnaissance team, they found the most strange

00:02:53.980 --> 00:03:02.470
and damning discovery yet: A creature who
would soon come to be known as SCP-6000-A.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:07.500
Much like the Exclusion Zone itself, she was
both terrifying and beautiful.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.510
Half maiden, half monster: A sleeping woman
with skin covered in scales, her legs replaced

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:16.680
by the huge, coiling tail of a python.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.130
She was reminiscent of the mythical nagas
or gorgons.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:25.310
And thankfully for all the MTF Members in
attendance, she was already in a sort of unconscious

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.760
state when they happened upon her.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.020
Perhaps even more bizarre than the creature
herself was the question of what on earth

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.110
had been going on around her.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:39.030
She was found at the base of a mutated tree,
already half-way transformed into a bookcase.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:44.660
There were several large stones placed in
a circle around her, each one covered in thaumaturgic

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:50.450
symbols, written in what was presumably human
blood, as though some strange ritual had recently

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.069
been performed.

00:03:52.069 --> 00:03:57.780
But the most notable detail of all was the
fact that she was surrounded by twelve corpses.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:02.630
Though they remain unidentified to this day,
there is one thing about the corpses that

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:06.490
was clear: They were all wearing dark robes.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:10.411
It didn’t take the Foundation’s brightest
minds to calculate the connection: Figures

00:04:10.411 --> 00:04:15.580
in robes performing strange rituals, anomalous
creatures everywhere, the sudden appearance

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:21.940
of a multitude of anomalous books, and a decidedly
serpentine entity - This had to be somehow

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:27.160
related to The Serpent’s Hand, an infamous
group of interest that takes a hard stance

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:32.240
on defying the Foundation’s secrecy, and
revealing the truth about anomalies to the

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:33.240
world.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:39.800
While still comatose, SCP-6000-A was secured,
and taken back to be contained in Facility-57,

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.889
while Sigma-3 Fireteam Chicago suited up and
prepared for their first trip into the Exclusion

00:04:44.889 --> 00:04:46.000
Zone.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.120
Among them, of course, was Site-19 Director
Tilda Moose.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.830
So, let’s answer the big question on everyone’s
mind right now: Why send someone as important

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:59.820
as a Site Director into a potentially hazardous
environment, where an entire reconnaissance

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:05.410
team had already gone missing, and the Serpent’s
Hand were believed to still be operating?

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:10.870
Because nobody at the Foundation understands
the Serpent’s Hand quite like Director Moose.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:16.419
After all, in another life, before joining
the SCP Foundation, she worked with them.

00:05:16.419 --> 00:05:21.830
To the Foundation, cooperative ex-GOI members
are worth their weight in gold, and in a situation

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:26.720
like this, nobody could possibly be more valuable
than Director Moose.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.420
She knew The Way, and we mean that literally.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:36.320
All signs pointed to SCP-6000 being the largest
and most unstable Class-W Gateway that the

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:41.820
Foundation has ever discovered, but you might
be more familiar with the concept of Class-W

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:47.419
Gateways when they’re referred to simply
as “The Ways” - the entrances into and

00:05:47.419 --> 00:05:53.180
out of The Wanderer’s Library, the legendary
extra-dimensional location that exists as

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:58.210
a nexus between various realities that has
become a kind of unofficial headquarters for

00:05:58.210 --> 00:05:59.800
the Serpent’s Hand.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:04.400
Director Moose had used the Ways before to
enter and gain access to the Wanderer’s

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.650
Library back when she was one of the Hands,
but even she admitted that she’d never seen

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.820
anything quite like SCP-6000.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.830
When they touched down and Director Moose
first saw the environment around them - the

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:22.100
shelves, the books, the pages, the text - she
felt a powerful sense of deja vu.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:26.580
It was just like the library, but something
was wrong.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:32.860
The Way here was not a standard dimensional
doorway, it was more like a kind of open wound,

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:37.100
and the Wanderer’s Library was spilling
out of it and into our world.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:42.070
Given the raw cosmic power of the Library,
it was bound to twist our world into its own

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:45.199
image, not the other way around.

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:49.949
There was only one thing to do from here:
With Director Moose leading at the head, they

00:06:49.949 --> 00:06:54.930
would use this vast new Way to travel into
the heart of the Wanderer’s Library itself,

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:56.710
and search for answers.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:02.460
After all, the Library is said to contain
all knowledge across the multiverse, so presumably,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:07.919
it would grant them the solution they needed
to the SCP-6000 dilemma they were faced with.

00:07:07.919 --> 00:07:13.430
But getting answers from the library, especially
if you work for the SCP Foundation, is easier

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.180
said than done.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:20.730
As Fireteam Chicago made their way further
into SCP-6000, they reached an area that Director

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:24.669
Moose could at least recognize as the true
Wanderer’s Library.

00:07:24.669 --> 00:07:28.110
The dirt gave way to wood paneling beneath
their feet.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:30.770
The air shimmered like an oil slick.

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:36.660
Mutated trees gave way to giant, empty bookcases,
like they were witnessing the birth of a new

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:37.660
expansion.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:41.210
As soon as they arrived, and began venturing
through the labyrinthine corridors of the

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:45.729
Wanderer’s Library, the library’s inhabitants
took notice.

00:07:45.729 --> 00:07:48.600
Surprised that the library has some permanent
residents?

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:53.729
Don’t be - after all, you can hardly have
a library without librarians.

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:58.200
As only people with experience like Director
Moose know, the Wanderer’s Library has an

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:03.199
enormous contingent of creatures known as
librarians and archivists permanently wandering

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:08.380
their halls, arranging the books and protecting
the location from threats.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:10.580
Threats like Fireteam Chicago.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:14.110
The team had their weapons at the ready when
they first saw the creatures appearing all

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:15.160
around them.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:19.750
Director Moose noticed a giant, red millipede
skittering through the hall past them.

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:20.750
An Archivist.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:25.660
It didn’t appear actively threatening, but
its presence already was a bad sign.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:29.380
Then, a feminine, hissing voice rang out through
the air.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:32.849
It said, “I see your intentions.

00:08:32.849 --> 00:08:34.019
Begone.”

00:08:34.019 --> 00:08:37.800
Something about it communicated a dark and
infinite power.

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.999
And with that, more nightmarish librarians
entered the scene.

00:08:40.999 --> 00:08:47.110
Huge, spider-like beings with countless limbs
- Class-3 Librarians - began crawling down

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.070
the bookcases towards them.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:53.980
But there was something different about these
creatures, something even worse: They were

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:57.490
wearing the uniform of the reconnaissance
team.

00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:01.730
In fact, once upon a time, they were the reconnaissance
team.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:07.590
But their stories had taken a drastic turn
since they got involved with SCP-6000, something

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:11.389
the rest of the world would soon be able to
relate to.

00:09:11.389 --> 00:09:16.269
Just seeing this terrified Moose: It wasn’t
unheard of for the Library to transform people

00:09:16.269 --> 00:09:20.740
into librarians, but that was typically a
punishment reserved for people who commit

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:24.300
only the gravest offenses against the anomaly.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:28.529
What had caused the Wanderer’s Library to
become so actively aggressive?

00:09:28.529 --> 00:09:32.029
They didn’t have time to think about it
because things were getting worse.

00:09:32.029 --> 00:09:37.029
As the Class-3 librarians reached the bottom
of the bookcase, they began to give chase,

00:09:37.029 --> 00:09:40.660
leading to Fireteam Chicago desperately fleeing
the scene.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.509
But they weren’t fast enough.

00:09:42.509 --> 00:09:46.819
The creatures began to attack, tackling and
slaughtering members of the team.

00:09:46.819 --> 00:09:52.200
The survivors fired back, but this led to
even more aggression from the Librarians.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.699
Soon, a group of Class-2 Librarians - giant,
hooded beings wielding lanterns - began to

00:09:56.699 --> 00:09:57.910
join the chase.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:02.139
What started as a highly-organized mission
soon became a massacre.

00:10:02.139 --> 00:10:06.629
The Foundation operatives were hopelessly
outnumbered by the Library’s legion of obedient

00:10:06.629 --> 00:10:07.629
monsters.

00:10:07.629 --> 00:10:10.139
They could not fight and win.

00:10:10.139 --> 00:10:13.830
It was unlike anything they’d ever seen
before.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:17.819
Director Moose was lucky to escape with her
life, and in response to the newly-assessed

00:10:17.819 --> 00:10:23.470
severity of the SCP-6000 threat, a wider barrage
of containment methods - known as Project

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:25.629
FUSILLADE - was initiated.

00:10:25.629 --> 00:10:30.709
This meant making Facility-57 the official
containment site of SCP-6000.

00:10:30.709 --> 00:10:35.199
This also resulted in the deployment of active
arrest warrants put on all active Serpent’s

00:10:35.199 --> 00:10:40.319
Hand cells, as well as giving MTF Sigma-3
blanket permissions to aggressively use any

00:10:40.319 --> 00:10:44.379
means of force against the Wanderer’s Library’s
growing aggression.

00:10:44.379 --> 00:10:46.569
But trouble was already brewing back at Facility-57.

00:10:46.569 --> 00:10:53.819
SCP-6000-A had awakened, and attempted to
attack the Foundation personnel around it.

00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:59.879
When it - or rather, she - was safely recontained,
Director Moose volunteered to interview her

00:10:59.879 --> 00:11:01.319
first-hand.

00:11:01.319 --> 00:11:04.699
She hoped that the creature might at least
have some information about what was going

00:11:04.699 --> 00:11:07.259
on with SCP-6000.

00:11:07.259 --> 00:11:13.239
When the interrogation began, 6000-A referred
to Director Moose as a Jailor - the Serpent’s

00:11:13.239 --> 00:11:16.579
Hand expression for members of the SCP Foundation.

00:11:16.579 --> 00:11:21.869
From this, Moose could easily recognize that
6000-A had once been a member of the Hand.

00:11:21.869 --> 00:11:27.860
A human, too, but she’d been changed into
her current form by a far more powerful being:

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:33.649
The Serpent, the great entity that rules the
Wanderer’s Library, and the very namesake

00:11:33.649 --> 00:11:35.269
of the Serpent’s Hand.

00:11:35.269 --> 00:11:41.239
A being as old as the universe, the queen
of all knowledge, and a kind of God in her

00:11:41.239 --> 00:11:42.239
own right.

00:11:42.239 --> 00:11:46.529
In a sense, Director Moose had a standing
grudge with the Serpent: Back when she was

00:11:46.529 --> 00:11:51.720
a member of the Hand, Moose was thirsty for
knowledge, she craved stories, but always

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:54.860
frustrated by the fact that they had to end.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:58.610
She stole sacred texts from the archive of
the Wanderer’s Library, and ensured that

00:11:58.610 --> 00:12:01.339
she would never truly be welcome again.

00:12:01.339 --> 00:12:06.499
As the interview ended, 6000-A promised to
continue looking through the stories of the

00:12:06.499 --> 00:12:11.339
library, hoping that the answer to their struggles
would perhaps be hidden within.

00:12:11.339 --> 00:12:17.259
Meanwhile, as SCP-6000 continued its aggressive
expansion through the Amazon, mutating and

00:12:17.259 --> 00:12:22.629
consuming more land, the efforts of Project
FUSILLADE subsequently escalated as well.

00:12:22.629 --> 00:12:28.379
In one of the largest coordinated efforts
in Foundation history, Operation BLACKSTAR,

00:12:28.379 --> 00:12:33.959
Mobile Task Forces on every continent conducted
a simultaneous raid of every single known

00:12:33.959 --> 00:12:36.329
Serpent’s Hand hotspot worldwide.

00:12:36.329 --> 00:12:38.959
And they found...nothing.

00:12:38.959 --> 00:12:41.179
Every single base was empty.

00:12:41.179 --> 00:12:45.399
All over the world, not a single member of
the Serpent’s Hand could be found.

00:12:45.399 --> 00:12:49.060
It was roundly concluded that they’d all
returned through the world’s various Ways

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:54.170
into the Wanderer’s Library, like a mother
ship that was calling them all home.

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:58.370
The Serpent’s Hand, the only people who
might have known what was coming, were escaping

00:12:58.370 --> 00:12:59.540
the planet.

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:03.490
As far as bad signs go, this was probably
the worst one possible.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:06.129
The proverbial “rats fleeing the ship.”

00:13:06.129 --> 00:13:11.220
And if the Library wanted its servants to
leave the Earth, it stood to reason that the

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:15.869
Serpent had something horrible planned for
everyone and everything left on it.

00:13:15.869 --> 00:13:20.629
Not long after this, Director Moose decided
to deliver a grim statement to the O5 Council

00:13:20.629 --> 00:13:26.420
about SCP-6000, informing them of the gravity
of this new situation.

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:28.639
“I don't know what precipitated this.

00:13:28.639 --> 00:13:33.360
But if the Library’s turned against humanity,
the situation is going to be dire.

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:38.019
It's a completely alien entity to us, we can't
even begin to understand why it is or does

00:13:38.019 --> 00:13:39.149
the things it does.

00:13:39.149 --> 00:13:42.459
Hell, we don't even know if there's a rhyme
or reason to anything.

00:13:42.459 --> 00:13:47.290
Most Foundation personnel only know of the
Library in the abstract, but I and a handful

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:52.109
of others have actually stepped foot in the
shelves and we can tell you that it is humanity's

00:13:52.109 --> 00:13:56.700
greatest blessing and its greatest threat
all at the same time.

00:13:56.700 --> 00:14:00.549
Imagine how much history has been influenced
by people getting into the Library and bringing

00:14:00.549 --> 00:14:03.239
back knowledge they would otherwise never
have had.

00:14:03.239 --> 00:14:07.379
Now imagine if the source of all knowledge
in the universe turns on us.

00:14:07.379 --> 00:14:10.170
I don't see this story having a happy ending.”

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:13.870
In spite of her grim prognosis, Director Moose
couldn’t give up.

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:19.480
In fact, she was relieved of her duties as
Site-19 Director in order to pursue the SCP-6000

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:25.399
case and subvert the library apocalypse full-time,
seeing as it seemed she was the only high-ranking

00:14:25.399 --> 00:14:29.949
member of Foundation personnel who truly understood
what was going on.

00:14:29.949 --> 00:14:33.730
She and the rest of the Project FUSILLADE
incident committee scrambled to find ways

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:38.829
to destroy SCP-6000, or at least put a halt
to its rampant growth.

00:14:38.829 --> 00:14:43.449
They sent in legions of Mobile Task Forces
with flame and heat-based weaponry, trying

00:14:43.449 --> 00:14:45.800
to burn down all the mutated trees.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:50.670
However, the trees showed an almost complete
resistance to this method, with burnt matter

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:52.879
quickly regenerating after the fact.

00:14:52.879 --> 00:14:57.149
In an even more savage act of war against
the Wanderer’s Library itself, they fired

00:14:57.149 --> 00:15:00.110
a high-yield explosive into the mouth of SCP-6000.

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:05.680
However, the second the bomb passed the threshold
into the Library, its effects were immediately

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:06.929
nullified.

00:15:06.929 --> 00:15:11.619
These concerning developments led to Moose
and her team finally pulling out the big gun.

00:15:11.619 --> 00:15:16.199
Literally, the biggest gun there is: a giant
network of defensive satellites capable of

00:15:16.199 --> 00:15:19.389
producing a massively destructive laser beam
blast from space, that is only called in for

00:15:19.389 --> 00:15:24.119
the most dire situations due to its huge potential
for collateral damage.

00:15:24.119 --> 00:15:30.019
However, when they fired the Array’s beam
down onto SCP-6000 at full power, it remained

00:15:30.019 --> 00:15:32.019
utterly unaffected.

00:15:32.019 --> 00:15:36.359
These were just three of 34 different failed
attempts by Project FUSILLADE to put an end

00:15:36.359 --> 00:15:41.720
to the Library’s rampage before it could
keep expanding, and eventually destroy the

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:42.720
earth.

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:47.869
In a fit of desperation, Moose returned once
again to SCP-6000-A, hoping she would have

00:15:47.869 --> 00:15:49.350
more answers.

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:53.149
They needed a miracle to stop this thing from
becoming an XK-Class Scenario.

00:15:53.149 --> 00:15:56.939
At first, the imprisoned snake-woman was cryptic.

00:15:56.939 --> 00:16:02.239
Instead of giving Moose useful information,
she told her the true origin of the Serpent’s

00:16:02.239 --> 00:16:07.379
Hand: They were once a cult devoted to the
Serpent herself, warriors on behalf of knowledge

00:16:07.379 --> 00:16:08.449
incarnate.

00:16:08.449 --> 00:16:12.859
The modern Serpent’s Hand, which claims
to be comparatively secular, is little more

00:16:12.859 --> 00:16:18.810
than a shadow of what it used to be, but they
still perform the same basic task: Destroying

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:21.069
secrecy, and advancing knowledge.

00:16:21.069 --> 00:16:25.430
As Moose’s patience wore thin, she asked
the Snake whether she had any specific stories

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:28.699
about how the Foundation could beat a Library
gone mad.

00:16:28.699 --> 00:16:31.919
But the Snake told her there were no such
stories.

00:16:31.919 --> 00:16:36.040
She did, however, surrender one vital piece
of information, saying:

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:41.149
“All the stories I see with the Foundation
staring down the end of the world…

00:16:41.149 --> 00:16:43.589
They have the Bookburners by their side.

00:16:43.589 --> 00:16:46.220
I suppose there's something to say there.

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:50.959
Rivals, allying themselves against a threat
greater than either could imagine.

00:16:50.959 --> 00:16:52.179
Smoking ash.

00:16:52.179 --> 00:16:55.499
A fusillade of fire against a wall of trees…”

00:16:55.499 --> 00:16:57.129
There it was.

00:16:57.129 --> 00:17:02.999
“Fusillade” - the namesake of Moose’s
Project against SCP-6000, which the Snake

00:17:02.999 --> 00:17:05.620
would have had no way of knowing about.

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:06.990
This was it.

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:11.100
This was the one narrative thread that could
lead them to their salvation.

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:16.079
But there was one other detail that caught
Moose’s well-trained eye, as well: The Bookburners.

00:17:16.079 --> 00:17:21.820
Better known to the SCP Foundation, and to
you, as the Global Occult Coalition, one of

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:26.670
the Foundation’s most bitter rivals in the
field of combating the strange.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:31.360
But this time, would it be worth putting aside
their differences to face the greatest threat

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:33.130
in their shared history?

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:38.100
Could the Foundation and the GOC together
be the Fusillade that holds back the Library’s

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:40.790
terrifying advance, once and for all?

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:45.660
The O5 Council held a vote on whether the
Foundation should get the GOC involved.

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:50.000
After much deliberation, the motion was approved
by a narrow three-vote margin.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:55.380
An emergency envoy from the Foundation was
immediately sent to GOC headquarters in Germany

00:17:55.380 --> 00:18:00.810
to invite them to become a part of Project
FUSILLADE, and stop SCP-6000’s expansion

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:02.900
before it was too late.

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:06.850
After comprehending the full scope of the
situation, and finally coming to understand

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:13.010
just how incredibly dangerous SCP-6000 was,
the leaders of the GOC approved this historic

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.490
high-level partnership.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:20.370
GOC PHYSICS , PSYCHE, and PTOLEMY division
personnel were transported to Facility-57

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:22.860
for briefing on the developing situation.

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:27.481
If they were lucky, they weren’t too late
to stop this XK-Class End of the World Scenario

00:18:27.481 --> 00:18:29.660
from unfolding into completion.

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:35.640
Interestingly, the GOC, prior to the SCP-6000
incident, had actually conducted more successful

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:39.630
raids into the Wanderer’s Library than even
the Foundation had.

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:45.130
So it was natural that, soon after they became
officially involved, the O5 Council and GOC

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:50.670
Command authorized another mission into the
library with GOC Strike Team 9842 "Probable

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:56.020
Cause", codenamed “HARBOR”, in order to
discover more about the situation from within.

00:18:56.020 --> 00:19:03.170
Director Moose and one of her closest associates,
Agent Adam Macmillan, another former-Serpent’s-Hand-turned-Foundation-Agent,

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:06.640
would instruct the team via live video and
audio link.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:11.880
Supplied with state of the art GOC and Foundation
equipment, The HARBOR team entered the Exclusion

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:12.880
Zone.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:17.880
They couldn’t help but remark on the extraordinary
sights before them: A shimmering, technicolor

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:22.450
fantasy, filled with trees half-way transformed
into bookcases.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:27.020
But strangely, all the books within them were
utterly blank.

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:31.750
Following the instructions delivered by Moose
and Macmillan, the HARBOR team advanced further,

00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:37.360
until they found an area where the forest
properly converged into the Library itself.

00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:41.520
Much like Fireteam Chicago had been, the HARBOR
Strike Team was baffled to find that this

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:46.400
new, expanding wing of the library didn’t
seem to have any books inside its endless

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:47.480
bookcases.

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:52.740
The team remained discreet, as roving Librarians
patrolled the halls, and skittered-about high

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:53.940
above them.

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:57.570
Security in The Wanderer’s Library had grown
exponentially.

00:19:57.570 --> 00:20:03.770
Eventually, HARBOR found the first book of
the new wing: A slim volume, entitled simply,

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:05.410
“IN PROGRESS.”

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:09.010
Much like SCP-6000’s takeover of the world
outside.

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:13.260
But before they could relay the knowledge
within the book back to Moose and Macmillan,

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:16.540
the voice of the Serpent herself once again
sounded.

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:20.740
She asked the two members of the HARBOR Strike
Team why they were there, and when they claimed

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:25.560
to simply be following orders from their commanders,
the all-powerful knowledge deity accused them

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:26.580
of lying.

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:29.400
The strike team members trembled in fear.

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:34.220
Moments later, the two operatives were claimed
by the Library, much like the SCP Foundation

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:36.180
recon team before them.

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:39.530
The signal link between HARBOR and command
was severed.

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:41.430
They were gone.

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:46.770
The Foundation and the GOC’s first joint
venture against SCP-6000 had been useless.

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:51.110
But that wasn’t the worst part: The Library’s
unprecedented counter-attack was about to

00:20:51.110 --> 00:20:56.790
ruin everything that the two groups had been
trying to achieve over their entire existence.

00:20:56.790 --> 00:20:59.880
SCP-6000 began putting out radio signals.

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:05.850
But it wasn’t just 6000 itself: Every single
abandoned Serpent’s Hand cell, all over

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:10.410
the globe, suddenly began transmitting and
boosting the signal as well.

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:14.760
It was too big and too coordinated for even
the combined efforts of the Foundation and

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:17.000
the Coalition to get a handle on.

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:24.100
The signal was the voice of the Serpent herself,
hissing “The Garden is the Serpent’s Place.”

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:26.600
And a lot of people heard it.

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:31.280
Governments, organizations, and individuals
all over the world picked up the signal, and

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:34.810
its ultimate source: The Amazon Rainforest.

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:38.990
As millions of people began to turn their
attention to the Amazon, their eyes were drawn

00:21:38.990 --> 00:21:46.040
to one thing: SCP-6000, and its enormous,
shimmering oil slick expanding out over the

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:47.040
canopy.

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:50.820
Too big now for the Foundation and the GOC
to keep under wraps anymore.

00:21:50.820 --> 00:21:55.930
The internet blew up with millions of posts
and messages across every platform imaginable.

00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:59.050
Even the Foundation’s web crawler was utterly
overwhelmed.

00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:00.140
One forum poster said:

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:02.480
“You guys seen the pictures coming out of
Brazil?

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:06.520
MSNs probably gonna get a hold of it by tomorrow
and suppress the story, but it's insane.

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:09.940
I have no clue what the hell is going on in
the Amazon but the pictures make it look like

00:22:09.940 --> 00:22:11.180
some sort of oil slick.

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:12.990
It's kind of beautiful.”

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:13.990
Another commented:

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:15.580
“Yep, turns out I'm not nuts.

00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:18.510
My friends have been hearing it on their car
radios and HAMs too.

00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:23.960
A woman's voice, sort of nasally, repeating
numbers - coordinates, I think - and the words

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:26.430
"The garden is the serpent's place".

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:29.340
Sounds biblical - maybe a passphrase of some
sort.

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:33.610
Now, here's the kicker - if you look on Google
earth or anything for the coordinates, everythings

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:35.530
fine, it doesn't show anything out of the
ordinary.

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:40.100
But if you use a backdoor to get into the
wildlife observation cameras scattered around…

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:43.950
The cameras went offline immediately after,
but I managed to save this image.

00:22:43.950 --> 00:22:45.600
Starting to think I should go down there.

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:46.600
This could be big.”

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:49.820
But random people on the internet were just
the tip of the iceberg.

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:54.890
It didn’t take long for all the classified
information about SCP-6000 to fall into the

00:22:54.890 --> 00:22:57.800
hands of a number of mainstream media outlets.

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:02.050
The Wall Street Journal ran it with the headline,
“THE AMAZON ANOMALY.”

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:06.900
Not long after, The Guardian released, “Brazil
Under Lock Down”, as the efforts of the

00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:11.960
Foundation and the GOC to keep a lid on things
started to look more like a military coup

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:14.480
than a routine quarantine effort.

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:16.610
The horrors started to snowball.

00:23:16.610 --> 00:23:21.210
News about the strange activity in the Amazon
led to a massive influx of tourists and amateur

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:23.400
investigators attempting to gain access.

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:27.920
While the Foundation and the GOC did what
they could to hold people off, and provide

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:31.750
amnestics to those who were actually exposed,
it wasn’t enough.

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:36.700
The cat was out of the bag, and now, any attempts
to cover up what had already been released

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:40.000
felt like just another element of the conspiracy.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:44.590
Knowledge was spreading fast, and in trying
to cover it up, both the Foundation and the

00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:49.440
GOC risked exposing even their own existences
to the public in the process.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:53.080
The Serpent’s plan had been working perfectly,
it seemed.

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:57.840
But this was more than a mere compromise of
informational security: The increased global

00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:03.740
knowledge of SCP-6000 seemed to massively
increase its expansion, as though it fed on

00:24:03.740 --> 00:24:05.130
people’s knowledge of it.

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:10.910
The Exclusion Zone grew by a terrifying six
kilometers, and the mutations within its boundaries

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:13.340
only seemed to intensify.

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:17.860
Things had gotten so severe that the Coalitions
nuclear resources were being considered as

00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:21.130
a response to SCP-6000’s aggression.

00:24:21.130 --> 00:24:26.180
Not long after this, Director Moose’s closest
confidant within Project FUSILLADE, Agent

00:24:26.180 --> 00:24:27.970
Macmillan, disappeared.

00:24:27.970 --> 00:24:33.490
Security footage of Facility-57 showed him
performing a thaumaturgic ritual circle, similar

00:24:33.490 --> 00:24:39.550
to the one that had produced SCP-6000-A. The
cameras captured an intense flash of light

00:24:39.550 --> 00:24:43.430
from the circle, and afterwards, Macmillan
was gone.

00:24:43.430 --> 00:24:46.610
He left a note for Director Moose, saying:
“Sorry, Tilly.

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:51.140
I used to spend weeks amongst the Library's
shelves, perusing everything I could come

00:24:51.140 --> 00:24:52.140
across.

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:55.350
It was otherworldly, in the most literal sense
of the word.

00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:58.100
I'm sure you understand the feeling.

00:24:58.100 --> 00:25:01.700
The difference is that you couldn't go in,
you made the choice between the Library and

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:05.220
Foundation - when I first entered, it was
just a mission.

00:25:05.220 --> 00:25:10.280
I experienced something amazing, something
well beyond what I thought was ever possible.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:14.240
And I thought it was a shame that we looked
at something so magnificent and only worried

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:15.820
about how it might hurt us.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:17.990
I can see now that's not the case.

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:21.760
The Librarians are only hurting the people
that try to stop the expansion.

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:24.190
I have no intention of doing that.

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:28.630
This is the end of the story, not the one
where we somehow come up with a silver bullet

00:25:28.630 --> 00:25:29.990
to fix everything.

00:25:29.990 --> 00:25:32.520
The only thing I can do is change how I feel
about it.

00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:35.880
I just want to sit amongst the shelves and
see the false-stars again.

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:38.570
I think this is my happy ending.”

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:41.180
Director Moose certainly didn’t see it that
way.

00:25:41.180 --> 00:25:46.500
In spite of everything, she was still desperately
searching for that elusive silver bullet - the

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:52.410
secret to stopping SCP-6000 and reversing
the horrific damage it had already wrought.

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:57.350
They were the SCP Foundation, they always
found a solution, it was just a matter of

00:25:57.350 --> 00:25:59.130
looking harder for it.

00:25:59.130 --> 00:26:05.420
She returned once again to her last potential
resource on the matter: SCP-6000-A, that cryptic

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:11.150
snake woman they’d first discovered in the
initial recon missions into SCP-6000.

00:26:11.150 --> 00:26:19.130
In many ways, SCP-6000-A echoed the sentiments
of Agent Macmillan: To deny true fate is futile;

00:26:19.130 --> 00:26:22.520
just this once, the Foundation has run out
of silver bullets.

00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:30.070
Their end is coming, perfect and inevitable,
and it will happen at the hands of the Library

00:26:30.070 --> 00:26:31.470
and The Serpent.

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:35.720
She told Director Moose that you don’t get
to choose how your stories end.

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:38.110
Only how you perceive the ending.

00:26:38.110 --> 00:26:40.460
“This story,” she said.

00:26:40.460 --> 00:26:43.980
“Ends with two people sitting in a room,
talking.

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:46.750
And then they vanish into thin air.”

00:26:46.750 --> 00:26:51.590
Tired of the snake-woman’s cryptic nonsense,
Director Moose decided she was wasting her

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:52.590
time.

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:57.350
Determined, she, alongside the Foundation
and the Global Occult Coalition, would find

00:26:57.350 --> 00:26:59.300
a way to stop this thing.

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:05.710
They would end SCP-6000, reverse the damage
it had caused, and save the day.

00:27:05.710 --> 00:27:08.930
Unfortunately, this time, they couldn’t.

00:27:08.930 --> 00:27:14.180
The entire Amazon basin was quarantined by
Project FUSILLADE, but it didn’t matter.

00:27:14.180 --> 00:27:18.800
SCP-6000 just kept growing and growing and
growing.

00:27:18.800 --> 00:27:24.810
Soon after, a BM-Class Broken Masquerade Scenario
transpired, meaning that the Foundation and

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:29.760
the GOC were no longer able to contain the
secrets of this anomaly, as more and more

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:34.050
of the Western Hemisphere was consumed by
SCP-6000.

00:27:34.050 --> 00:27:39.480
The anomaly’s object class was upgraded
from Keter to Apollyon, as GOC Command and

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:45.090
the O5 Council realized that the ship had
officially sailed on it’s fight for containment.

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:48.080
And for the future of planet earth.

00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:52.900
As the Wanderer’s Library continued to consume
more of the planet in the following days,

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:58.350
the priorities of Project FUSILLADE shifted
focus from containing SCP-6000, to getting

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:03.650
as many key figures and anomalies as possible
off of earth or out of our dimension.

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:09.170
All population centers, GOC bases, and Foundation
Sites were considered lost.

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:10.470
It was over.

00:28:10.470 --> 00:28:12.710
The Serpent had won.

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:16.350
One person who didn’t flee with all the
others, was Director Moose.

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:22.470
With the prognosis being that SCP-6000 would
consume the entire world in the next two weeks,

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:28.240
Moose decided to go down with the ship, spending
her last days on a dying earth with her final

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:31.850
prisoner: SCP-6000-A.

00:28:31.850 --> 00:28:35.870
The snake-woman tried to comfort Director
Moose, in the face of the end.

00:28:35.870 --> 00:28:40.600
She told her that all there really is in life
is a collection of stories.

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:44.230
One ending is the beginning to countless others.

00:28:44.230 --> 00:28:50.250
The end of this earth, this Foundation, would
simply unravel unto more, splitting like a

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:53.720
thread’s end into infinite narrative possibilities.

00:28:53.720 --> 00:28:58.770
If Moose could only accept this, she would
not see this as an ending, but a wonderful

00:28:58.770 --> 00:28:59.840
new beginning.

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:02.490
A possibility for more stories.

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:06.530
That’s why the Library was making all these
new empty bookcases.

00:29:06.530 --> 00:29:12.380
There would soon be an abundance of new knowledge
to accommodate and fill it’s shelves.

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:18.390
Still, Moose seemed mournful about what she
saw as the end of her story, the end of the

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:19.390
world.

00:29:19.390 --> 00:29:24.090
She asked 6000-A whether her attempts to stop
everything had been pointless from the start,

00:29:24.090 --> 00:29:27.160
if this ending had always been set in stone.

00:29:27.160 --> 00:29:31.770
6000-A responded, “The point was the same
thing that it's ever been.

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:34.600
To make new stories where there was once nothing.

00:29:34.600 --> 00:29:36.290
Be happy, Tilda.

00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:38.870
Your story is going to be remembered forever.”

00:29:38.870 --> 00:29:41.130
How do you think Tilda Moose responded?

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:45.710
Did she say, “You think people will read
about us?” or rather did she ask “What

00:29:45.710 --> 00:29:47.440
if I don't want new stories?”

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:50.100
We’ll give you a moment to think about it........

00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:51.110
Are you ready?

00:29:51.110 --> 00:29:57.010
The answer is… it doesn’t matter, because
regardless of what Director Moose said, it

00:29:57.010 --> 00:29:59.720
would have ended exactly the same way.

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:04.950
If this tale teaches you anything, it should
be that you really can’t choose your ending,

00:30:04.950 --> 00:30:07.559
you can only choose how you react to it.

00:30:07.559 --> 00:30:13.260
Director Moose looked up, and realized she
was no longer inside Facility-57 - she was

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:18.050
in the Wanderer’s Library, staring up at
an infinitude of shelves that contained an

00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:22.460
expanse of books that were both boundless
and immeasurable.

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:28.310
She was surrounded by people and entities
of all shapes and sizes, wearing robes, browsing

00:30:28.310 --> 00:30:33.320
the new collection, and she recognized all
of her old Foundation friends and colleagues

00:30:33.320 --> 00:30:34.360
among them.

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:39.110
Director Moose’s story had come to an end,
but what she had failed to grasp was that

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:41.500
“the end” didn’t just mean death.

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:44.240
It meant the start of something new.

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:49.290
New worlds, new dimensions, new canons, new
anomalies.

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:56.410
A vast infinity of stories, and from inside
the Wanderer’s Library, with everyone she’d

00:30:56.410 --> 00:30:59.170
ever known, she could enjoy them all.

00:30:59.170 --> 00:31:05.310
The Wanderer’s Library and the Serpent hadn’t
killed her, they had saved her.

00:31:05.310 --> 00:31:07.800
This was Director Moose’s happy ending.

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:09.600
And also the ending of SCP-6000.

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:14.050
But what if… this wasn’t the end.

00:31:14.050 --> 00:31:20.340
What if instead, Tilda Moose had chosen a
third option and responded to 6000-A’s comment

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:26.510
about her story being remembered forever not
with a question, but a statement.

00:31:26.510 --> 00:31:29.250
“Stories on stories”

00:31:29.250 --> 00:31:33.031
Perhaps if she had answered this way, then
she would have been swept away from the world

00:31:33.031 --> 00:31:35.800
that was dying around them to somewhere new.

00:31:35.800 --> 00:31:38.250
And not just one world, but many.

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:42.540
Where she could have the chance to see a utopian
version of Earth where anomalies were a part

00:31:42.540 --> 00:31:47.910
of everyday life, their powers a part of the
fabric of society, where disease and suffering

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:50.070
had become a thing of the past.

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:54.660
She may then have seen alternate versions
of her own story, where she remained a member

00:31:54.660 --> 00:31:57.420
of the Serpent’s Hand until the very end.

00:31:57.420 --> 00:32:01.430
Or even another where she was a member of
the O5 council.

00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:05.150
These were all stories running parallel to
hers and ours.

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:07.580
Real, yet untouchable.

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:12.330
All she could do was watch these realities,
knowing that she’d be unable to help or

00:32:12.330 --> 00:32:19.230
interact with the people she saw, just as
6000-A had only been able to observe ours.

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:25.410
But in the end, that was okay, because there
was always another story to move on to.

00:32:25.410 --> 00:32:30.720
Someone else’s story to become invested
in, something else incredible to see.

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:36.070
Because just like in the Wanderer’s Library,
the end of one story only means the start

00:32:36.070 --> 00:32:37.770
of so many others.

00:32:37.770 --> 00:32:41.170
Now go check out “SCP-5000 Why?

00:32:41.170 --> 00:32:46.520
- The Full Story Compilation” and “SCP
XK Class End of the World Scenarios Explained”

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:51.030
for more SCP Foundation tales about the end
of the world as we know it!

00:32:51.030 --> 00:32:53.100
If you can really call it an end, that is…

